WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.640
Hey, welcome to Fascinating Frontiers, Episode 105, coming to you on June 18th, 2026.

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:09.460
Some fascinating developments to cover today.

00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:16.000
An upgraded Arian 6 just lofted Europe's heaviest payload ever, 36 Amazon satellites riding

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:19.300
on boosters carrying 14 extra tons of propellant each.

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:27.740
The VA 269 flight lifted 36 Amazon Project Kuiper satellites on the most powerful Arian 6

00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:28.820
configuration yet.

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:33.800
Upgraded boosters each carried 14 tons more propellant than earlier versions.

00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:37.640
The launch marked Europe's heaviest single payload success to date.

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:42.700
Engineers will review telemetry to confirm performance margins before the next flight.

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:47.660
Watch for the next Arian 6 mission later this year to see whether the new booster design

00:00:47.660 --> 00:00:48.580
becomes standard.

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:54.320
While Europe pushes payload limits, commercial cargo flights to the ISS continue their

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:55.040
steady rhythm.

00:00:55.680 --> 00:01:01.360
The CRS-34 capsule splashed down after undocking from the International Space Station.

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:07.120
Four main parachutes deployed during reentry and the trunk was jettisoned before landing.

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:10.960
The mission delivered supplies and returned science samples.

00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:15.540
This flight continues the steady cadence of commercial resupply flights.

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:20.280
Next up is a preparation for the following Dragon Mission later this summer.

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:26.540
From routine station logistics we move to a striking view of two galaxy clusters caught in

00:01:26.540 --> 00:01:39.180
the act of merging. The image shows CL0016 plus 1609, also known as MAXJ0018.5 plus 1626.

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:42.200
One of the brightest x-ray clusters known.

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:47.460
X-ray data revealed the system is two clusters aligned along our line of sight

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:54.200
and in the process of merging, the observations add to extensive radio and x-ray studies of the

00:01:54.200 --> 00:02:00.080
object. Hubble's visible light view highlights the distorted galaxies within the colliding system.

00:02:00.740 --> 00:02:04.580
Follow-up papers will examine how the merger affects galaxy evolution.

00:02:05.380 --> 00:02:11.600
While clusters reshape galaxies on cosmic scales, researchers are refining which nearby worlds

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:17.440
might still be habitable. Researchers refine criteria for identifying potentially habitable worlds

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:24.220
around other stars. The updated framework incorporates atmospheric retention and stellar activity

00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:28.500
levels. It reduces the list of high priority targets for future telescopes.

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:33.320
The work helps observers allocate limited observation time more effectively.

00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:37.680
Scientists will test the model against new James Webb data releases.

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:43.600
Canada is also sharpening its focus on future lunar science through a major ESA commitment.

00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:49.700
Canada committed record funding to the European Space Agency securing two lunar exploration

00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:55.680
opportunities. The investment supports Canadian instruments and technologies on upcoming European

00:02:55.680 --> 00:03:00.980
landers. It strengthens collaboration on surface science and resource prospecting.

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:06.960
Program managers will now finalize instrument selections and timelines. Watch for the first

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:12.520
hardware integration milestones in the coming year. From lunar planning, we shift to an experiment

00:03:12.520 --> 00:03:18.980
that literally sends grape seeds into orbit. Hundreds of wine grape seeds from Texas A&amp;M AgriLife

00:03:18.980 --> 00:03:25.520
will spend roughly six months aboard the ISS. Cosmic radiation exposure will be studied after

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:31.440
the seeds return for planting. Researchers aim to produce the first wine from space exposed grape

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:37.880
seeds. The experiment tests how radiation alters seed genetics and growth. Results are expected

00:03:37.880 --> 00:03:43.660
after the sample's complete post-flight analysis. While seeds ride to orbit, blue origin is

00:03:43.660 --> 00:03:48.620
rebuilding the pad that will soon launch its own heavy-lift rocket. Work has begun on the

00:03:48.620 --> 00:03:54.360
launch pad damaged in a new gland explosion less than three weeks ago. The company targets a return

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:59.900
to flight by the end of the year. Engineers are assessing structural repairs and safety systems.

00:03:59.900 --> 00:04:05.400
The effort keeps the heavy-lift rocket on schedule for future national security and commercial

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:12.160
missions. Next milestone is completion of pad certification tests. From launch pad recovery,

00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:18.620
we turn to a new orbital refueling capability for the Space Force. The Pentagon awarded quantum

00:04:18.620 --> 00:04:25.820
space a contract to develop a fuel transfer spacecraft for the US Space Force. The vehicle is slated

00:04:25.820 --> 00:04:32.120
for delivery by 2028. The project will demonstrate propellant transfer between satellites and orbit.

00:04:32.740 --> 00:04:37.980
Success would extend satellite lifetimes and reduce launch mass requirements. Early design

00:04:37.980 --> 00:04:43.380
reviews are scheduled for later this year. Refueling satellites in orbit could complement faster

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:49.260
lunar lander development now being studied by ESA. The agency is studying ways to shorten

00:04:49.260 --> 00:04:55.160
development timelines for future moon landers. Faster cadence would support more frequent science

00:04:55.160 --> 00:05:01.360
and technology demonstrations. Planners are examining reusable elements and streamlined qualification

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:08.400
processes. The goal is to reduce the gap between missions while maintaining reliability. Concepts

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:14.560
studies will feed into the next ESA ministerial meeting. While timelines for lunar missions tighten,

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:21.340
astronomers are confronting a hot Jupiter whose atmosphere defies standard models. Picture a gas

00:05:21.340 --> 00:05:27.840
giant orbiting so close to its star that its day-side temperature exceeds 2000 degrees Celsius.

00:05:28.600 --> 00:05:33.880
Radiation from the star slams into the upper atmosphere, breaking apart molecules and driving

00:05:33.880 --> 00:05:38.200
a powerful day-to-night wind that can reach thousands of kilometers per hour.

00:05:38.920 --> 00:05:44.620
On the night side, the temperature drops sharply, allowing some of those vaporized materials to

00:05:44.620 --> 00:05:49.900
condense into clouds that the winds then carry back into daylight where they evaporate again

00:05:49.900 --> 00:05:56.860
in a continuous cycle. The whole process repeats on time scales of hours, not years, creating a global

00:05:56.860 --> 00:06:02.900
heat engine that astronomers can watch in real-time with infrared telescopes. Yet no current model

00:06:02.900 --> 00:06:08.340
fully explains why some of these worlds appear to be losing atmosphere at rates far higher than

00:06:08.340 --> 00:06:14.620
expected, while others hold onto theirs despite similar conditions. What hidden variable decides

00:06:14.620 --> 00:06:21.020
whether a hot Jupiter keeps or sheds its outer layers? From atmospheric puzzles, we return to our

00:06:21.020 --> 00:06:28.400
own solar system where Perseverance has now driven a marathon's distance on Mars. NASA's Perseverance

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:36.080
has now driven more than 26.2 miles across Jezero crater. The distance matches a terrestrial marathon

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:42.020
and sets a new record for the rover. Scientists are using the extended traverse to sample diverse

00:06:42.020 --> 00:06:47.600
rock units. The data will help reconstruct the crater's ancient lake history. The team will

00:06:47.600 --> 00:06:52.380
continue mapping the route toward the next sampling area. From surface to verses on Mars,

00:06:52.760 --> 00:06:58.440
we look at an orbital rescue mission for an aging observatory. Northrop Grumman's Pegasus will

00:06:58.440 --> 00:07:03.940
launch the spacecraft that will raise the Swift Observatory's altitude. The boost will extend

00:07:03.940 --> 00:07:09.760
the mission's operational life by countering atmospheric drag. The mission combines NASA,

00:07:09.760 --> 00:07:16.040
catalyst space, and Northrop Grumman expertise. Launches plan once the servicing vehicle completes

00:07:16.040 --> 00:07:21.760
final testing. Success would demonstrate a new approach to extending aging observatories.

00:07:22.380 --> 00:07:27.700
Extending observatory life pairs naturally with new public-private work on Mars technologies.

00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:34.020
NASA announced a public-private partnership with Relativity Space to advance Mars exploration.

00:07:34.580 --> 00:07:38.540
The collaboration will explore new technologies for future science missions.

00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:44.700
It builds on earlier work between the company and agency scientists. The partnership aims to

00:07:44.700 --> 00:07:50.660
lower costs while increasing payload capacity. Details on specific mission concepts will emerge

00:07:50.660 --> 00:07:55.560
in coming months. From Mars technology partnerships, we zoom out to the engineering

00:07:55.560 --> 00:08:01.900
challenge of stabilizing future space telescope mirrors. Precise control of telescope mirrors is

00:08:01.900 --> 00:08:07.780
emerging as a critical requirement for detecting small exoplanets. Vibrations and thermal

00:08:07.780 --> 00:08:13.880
changes can blur the tiny signals needed for atmospheric characterization. Engineers are developing

00:08:13.880 --> 00:08:19.440
new stabilization techniques for the next generation of space telescopes. The improvements will

00:08:19.440 --> 00:08:25.180
directly affect the search for Earth-like worlds. Prototype tests are already underway in laboratory

00:08:25.180 --> 00:08:30.960
settings. From high precision optics, we close with a simple naked eyesight visible tonight.

00:08:31.600 --> 00:08:36.240
After sunset, the moon will appear close to the sickle asterism in LEO.

00:08:36.840 --> 00:08:42.640
Observers can watch the pair low in the western sky before they set. The geometry offers a good

00:08:42.640 --> 00:08:48.520
chance to spot Earth shine on the lunar surface. Binoculars will reveal the stars of the sickle

00:08:48.520 --> 00:08:54.860
alongside the moon. Clear western horizons will give the best views. Keep an eye on the next dragon

00:08:54.860 --> 00:09:00.900
cargo mission as it prepares for launch later this summer. That covers today's space and science news.

